  <p>
    A span kind mcpd does not recognise under
    <code>openinference.span.kind</code> or
    <code>gen_ai.operation.name</code> falls to <code>generic</code> rather
    than being rejected. The full list of kinds is in the
    <a href="/docs/reference/data-model" class="link">data model</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Logs and metrics</h2>
  <p>
    Log records map by severity number, the way the specification prescribes:
    1 to 4 is trace, 5 to 8 debug, 9 to 12 info, 13 to 16 warn, 17 to 20 error,
    and anything above that is fatal. With no severity number, a record is
    treated as info. The message comes from <code>body.stringValue</code>, the
    logger name from the scope name, and <code>traceId</code> and
    <code>spanId</code> are kept when they are valid, which is what puts a log
    line next to the span that emitted it.
  </p>
  <p>
    For metrics, sums become counters and gauges become gauges. Histograms are
    recorded by their sum and count with the bucket boundaries preserved, so
    every figure mcpd shows comes from what the histogram actually carries. Exemplars are kept, which is what lets a spike on a chart open
    the run that caused it. Most tools discard them.
  </p>

  <h2>Ids, and W3C traceparent</h2>
  <p>
    mcpd uses OpenTelemetry's identifier shapes exactly: a trace id is 128 bits
    as 32 lowercase hex characters, a span id is 64 bits as 16. Keeping the wire
    format identical is what removes the translation layer. The all-zero id is
    reserved to mean "no trace" and is refused wherever it appears, including in
    ids mcpd generates itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    OTLP/JSON specifies hex for id fields, but several SDKs emit base64. mcpd
    accepts both and stores hex. A span whose ids are missing, the wrong length
    or all-zero is skipped and the rest of the batch is still written, because a
    collector that rejects ten thousand good spans over one bad one is a
    collector people turn off.
  </p>
  <p>
    Timestamps are nanoseconds since the Unix epoch. They exceed 2<sup>53</sup>,
    so OTLP/JSON sends them as strings, and mcpd parses them as strings. Reading
    them as floating point loses precision, which shows up as spans that appear
    to start microseconds apart when they did not. An
    <code>endTimeUnixNano</code> of <code>0</code> or absent means the span has
    not finished.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <code>traceparent</code> header is
    <code>version-traceid-parentid-flags</code>. mcpd parses it with the rules
    the specification sets: version <code>ff</code> is refused, version
    <code>00</code> must have exactly four fields, later versions may carry
    more, the ids must validate, and bit 0 of the flags byte is the sampled
    flag. A malformed header is never a reason to refuse a request. mcpd starts
    a new trace instead.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where this matters: the mcpd SDKs read an inbound <code>traceparent</code>
    and emit one on outbound calls, so a trace survives a service boundary. The
    OTLP endpoints take their ids from the payload, because your exporter has
    already put them in the spans. If a trace begins in a service you do not
    instrument with mcpd, pass its <code>traceparent</code> along and both
    halves land under one trace id.
  </p>

