  <h2>What OTLP cannot carry</h2>
  <p>
    OTLP has a shape for spans, logs and metrics. It has none for a machine
    score, a human's thumbs-down or an aspect note, and no vocabulary for the
    token detail a modern provider bills on. Two ways to send those:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>POST /v1/ingest</code>, mcpd's native batch endpoint, which carries
      the full data model in one request.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>POST /v1/api/scores</code> and <code>POST /v1/api/feedback</code>,
      which attach to a trace you have already sent over OTLP.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Mixing the two is normal and expected. Export spans with the OpenTelemetry
    SDK you already run, and post scores from your evaluation job. They join on
    the trace id. See
    <a href="/docs/evaluation" class="link">Scores and feedback</a> and the
    <a href="/docs/reference/api" class="link">HTTP API</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Before you expose the port</h2>
  <p>
    With no <code>--ingest-key</code> set, anyone who can reach 4317 can write
    to it, and with no <code>--read-key</code>, read every stored prompt. mcpd
    logs a warning at startup when it binds a non-loopback address with either
    left open. Set them with
    <a href="/docs/configuration" class="link">configuration</a>, then send the
    key as <code>Authorization: Bearer</code>, which is what
    <code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS</code> above does. Every door on this page
    reads the key the same way, gRPC metadata included.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="my-6"
    label="run with keys"
    command="mcpd --ingest-key $INGEST_KEY --read-key $READ_KEY"
  />
